"<table id=\"e1\"> (https://publications.cuni.cz/bitstream/handle/20.500.14178/2522/SODA24-119.pdf?isAllowed=y&sequence=1)\nciteturn4search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; **(b) Face distribution** ... **Conjecture 1.1. ... (Campion Loth and Mohar [6, Theorem 3])** *The expected number of faces in a random embedding of any graph is at most \\(\\frac{\\pi^2}{2}n\\).\n| \\(\\approx 2\\ln n\\) | 2 | 2.77 | 3.22 | 3.58 | 3.89 |\n\n</table>\n\nTable 1: Data obtained by exhaustive computation concerning \\(K_n\\) for \\(n\\leq 6\\).\n\nIt was proved by Grannell and Knor [16] (see also [14] and [15]) that for infinitely many values of \\(n\\) there is a constant \\(c>0\\) such that the number of embeddings with precisely \\(\\frac{1}{3}n(n-1)\\) faces is at least \\(n^{cn^2}\\). All these embeddings are triangular (all faces are triangles) and thus of minimum possible genus. When we compare this result with the fact that\n\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{Emb}(K_n)|=((n-2)!)^n=n^{\\Theta(n^2)},\n\\]\n\nwe see that there is huge abundance of embeddings of \\(K_n\\) with many more than logarithmically many faces.\n\nStahl [38] introduced the notion of *permutation-partition pairs* with which he was able to describe partially fixed rotation systems. Through the linearity of expectation these became a powerful tool to analyze what happens in average. In particular, he was able to prove that the expected number of faces in embeddings of complete graphs is much lower than quadratic.\n\n**Theorem 1.1. (Stahl [40, Corollary 2.3])** *The expected number of faces in a random embedding of the complete graph \\(K_n\\) is at most \\(n+\\ln n\\).*\n\nComputer simulations show that even the bound given in Theorem 1.1 is too high. In fact, Mauk and Stahl conjectured the following.\n\n**Conjecture 1.1. (Mauk and Stahl [32, page 289])** *The expected number of faces in a random embedding of the complete graph \\(K_n\\) is at most \\(2\\ln n+O(1)\\).*\n\nFor general graphs, a slightly weaker bound than that of Theorem 1.1 was derived by Stahl using the same approach as in [40]; it had appeared in [39] a couple of years earlier.\n\n**Theorem 1.2. (Stahl [39, Theorem 1])** *The expected number of faces in a random embedding of any \\(n\\)-vertex graph is at most \\(n\\ln n\\).*\n\nThe \\(n\\ln n\\) bound of Stahl was improved only recently. Campion Loth, Halasz, Masařík, Mohar, and Šámal [4] conjectured that the bound should be linear, which was then proved in [6].\n\n**Theorem 1.3. (Campion Loth and Mohar [6, Theorem 3])** *The expected number of faces in a random embedding of any graph is at most \\(\\frac{\\pi^2}{2}n\\).*\n\nThe bound of Theorem 1.3 is essentially best possible as there are \\(n\\)-vertex graphs whose expected number of faces is \\(\\frac{1}{3}n+1\\), see [6].\n\nCopyright © 2024 by SIAM  \nUnauthorized reproduction of this article is prohibited--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCMPP conjecture on sequenceability of zero-sum subsets without inverse pairs | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/373756/cmpp-conjecture-on-sequenceability-of-zero-sum-subsets-witho)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # CMPP conjecture on sequenceability of zero-sum subsets without inverse pairs ... Simone Costa, Stefano Della Fiore, Mattia Fontana and Lluís Vena, “Graham conjecture on small sets in abelian groups”, arXiv:2603.20961 (2026).\n\n# CMPP conjecture on sequenceability of zero-sum subsets without inverse pairs\n\nStatus\n    open\n\nImportance\n    Research corpus\n\nSolutions\n    0\n\nLess than 1 year old · traced to 2026\n\nLet $G$G be an abelian group, and let $A$A be a finite subset of $G\\setminus\\{0\\}$G∖{0}. An ordering $a_1,\\ldots,a_{|A|}$a 1​,…,a∣A∣​ of $A$A is sequenceable if its partial sums\n\n$$p_i=a_1+\\cdots+a_i$$p i​=a 1​+⋯+a i​\n\nare pairwise distinct and $p_i\\ne 0$p i​=0 for every $1\\leq i\\leq |A|-1$1≤i≤∣A∣−1. CMPP conjecture. Every subset $A\\subseteq G\\setminus\\{0\\}$A⊆G∖{0} whose elements sum to zero and such that $\\{x,-x\\}\\not\\subset A${x,−x}⊂A for every $x\\in G$x∈G is sequenceable. The conjecture concerns sequenceability in connection with Heffter arrays; the supplied text does not establish its general status, although it proves the claim for zero-sum subsets without inverse pairs of size at most $23$23.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nSimone Costa, Stefano Della Fiore, Mattia Fontana and Lluís Vena, “Graham conjecture on small sets in abelian groups”, arXiv:2603.20961 (2026).\n\nabelian-groupsadditive-combinatoricssequenceability\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRemarks on the conjectures of Capparelli, Meurman, Primc and Primc | Selecta Mathematica | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00029-026-01198-y)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 2 weeks ago;   * Published: 30 September 2026 ... Gordon’s partition theorem [20] is one of the deepest and most beautiful results in the theory of integer partitions, generalising the combinatorial version of the Rogers–Ramanujan identities to arbitrary odd moduli. ... Let $\\lambda $ $\\lambda $ be an integer partition, that is, $\\lambda =({\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2},\\dots )$ $\\lambda =(\\lambda _1,\\lambda _2,\\dots )$ is a weakly decreasing sequence of nonnegative integers such that only finitely many ${\\lambda }_{i}$ $\\lambda _i$ are strictly positive [3]. ... (CMPP [12, Conjecture 4.1]) For k, n nonnegative integers and ${k}_{0},\\dots ,{k}_{n}$ $k_0,\\dots ,k_n$ nonnegative integers such that ${k}_{0}+\\cdots +{k}_{n}=k$ $k_0+\\dots +k_n=k$, ... CMPP write about their conjecture that “for $n>1$ $n>1$ there is no obvious connection [...] with representation theory of affine Lie algebras.”\n\n# Remarks on the conjectures of Capparelli, Meurman, Primc and Primc\n\n  * Open access\n  * Published: 30 September 2026\n\n  * Volume 32, article number 100 (2026)\n  * Cite this article\n\nYou have full access to this open access article\n\nDownload PDF\n\n[Input] Save article\n\nView saved research\n\nSelecta Mathematica Aims and scope Submit manuscript (selecta@math.mit.edu)\n\nRemarks on the conjectures of Capparelli, Meurman, Primc and Primc\n\nDownload PDF\n\n  * Shashank Kanade^{1},\n  * Matthew C. Russell^{2},\n  * Shunsuke Tsuchioka^{3} &\n  * …\n  * S. Ole Warnaar^{4} \nShow authors\n\n  * 250 Accesses\n\n  * Explore all metrics\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn a series of two papers, S. Capparelli, A. Meurman, A. Primc, M. Primc (CMPP) and then M. Primc put forth three remarkable sets of conjectures, stating that the generating functions of coloured integer partitions in which the parts satisfy restrictions on the multiplicities admit simple infinite product forms. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLinear expected faces in random orientable embeddings — Graph-theory open problems (arXiv) (https://graph-theory-ai.github.io/graph-conjectures/arxiv/2103.05036__00/)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; Conjecture 1 from arXiv:2103.05036 is fully resolved by Campion Loth and Mohar (2023), who proved that for any $n$-vertex multigraph with maximum edge-multiplicity $\\mu$, the expected number of faces satisfies $E[F] \\leq n(H_{2\\mu}+1)$; for simple graphs this specialises to the conjectured $O(n)$ bound, further sharpened to $E[F] \\leq \\frac{\\pi^2}{6}n$ by the same paper. ...   * implied by Linear face bound in random graph embeddings open vacuous: the target is now a theorem Both statements concern the same quantity: the expected number of faces E[F] of a uniformly random orientable embedding (random rotation system) of an arbitrary n-vertex simple graph.\n\n# Linear expected faces in random orientable embeddings\n\nConjecture 1 · arXiv:2103.05036\n\narXiv Conjecture high confidence— first stated 2021-10-06\n\nAuthors: Jesse Campion Loth, Kevin Halasz, Tomáš Masařík, Bojan Mohar, Robert Šámal\n\n## Status solved high confidence\n\nConjecture 1 from arXiv:2103.05036 is fully resolved by Campion Loth and Mohar (2023), who proved that for any $n$-vertex multigraph with maximum edge-multiplicity $\\mu$, the expected number of faces satisfies $E[F] \\leq n(H_{2\\mu}+1)$; for simple graphs this specialises to the conjectured $O(n)$ bound, further sharpened to $E[F] \\leq \\frac{\\pi^2}{6}n$ by the same paper. Subsequent work (arXiv:2211.01032, SODA 2024) shows that for complete graphs and most natural graph families the expectation is in fact $\\Theta(\\ln n)$, far below the linear upper bound.\n\n### Cited literature (2)\n\n  * proof Expected number of faces in a random embedding of any graph is at most linear (2023)\n\nJesse Campion Loth, Bojan Mohar · Combinatorics, Probability and Computing · arXiv:2202.07746 · doi:10.1017/S096354832300010X\n\nProves Conjecture 1 by establishing $E[F] \\leq n(H_{2\\mu}+1)$ for any multigraph (Theorem 3), which specialises to $O(n)$ for simple graphs and is further sharpened to $E[F] \\leq \\frac{\\pi^2}{6}n$ by Theorem 8.\n\n  * partial Random Embeddings of Graphs: The Expected Number of Faces in Most Graphs is Logarithmic (2024)\n\nJesse Campion Loth, Kevin Halasz, Tomáš Masařík, Bojan Mohar, Robert Šámal · ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms (SODA 2024) · arXiv:2211.01032\n\nGoes beyond the resolved conjecture by proving $E[F(K_n)] = \\Theta(\\ln n)$ and logarithmic bounds for random graphs $G(n,p)$ and bounded-degree models, showing the O(n) bound is far from tight for most graphs.\n\nReviewer notes. Conjecture 1 is fully proved by arXiv:2202.07746. The three internal references reduce to two distinct papers (2202.07746 appears twice with fuzz scores 97 and 92). arXiv:2211.01032 strengthens the result considerably by showing logarithmic behaviour for most graphs.\n\nConjecture. For every $n$-vertex simple graph $G$, the expected number of faces when selecting an orientable embedding of $G$ uniformly at random is $O(n)$.\n\n## Context\n\nThe authors exhibit a general construction of graph families achieving a linear number of expected faces but were unable to find any family with a superlinear number of expected faces. Results for $d$-regular and $d$-degenerate graphs (Theorem 11 and Corollary 12) are presented as supporting evidence.\n\n## Source paper\n\nRandom 2-cell embeddings of multistars\nJesse Campion Loth, Kevin Halasz, Tomáš Masařík, Bojan Mohar, Robert Šámal · 2021-10-06\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2103.05036 PDF source\n\n## Related conjectures\n\n  * implied by Linear face bound in random graph embeddings open vacuous: the target is now a theorem Both statements concern the same quantity: the expected number of faces E[F] of a uniformly random orientable embedding (random rotation system) of an arbitrary n-vertex simple graph. The source's explicit bound E[F] <= n/3 + 1 is in particular O(n), which is exactly the target's assertion. Direction is correct: the source is the stronger, quantitatively sharp statement. Consistency check: the target is marked solved, and indeed the source paper (2202.07746) proved E[F] <= (pi^2/6)n, establishing the target while leaving the sharp constant 1/3 open.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGraham conjecture on small sets in abelian groups (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.20961)\nciteturn4academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Date: Sat Mar 21 21:57:00 2026 ... In this paper, using a recursive approach, we investigate the sequenceability of subsets $A$ in generic abelian groups for small values of $|A|$. ... Finally, regarding the related CMPP conjecture, we show that zero-sum subsets without inverse pairs are sequenceable for $|A| \\leq 23$.\nTitle: Graham conjecture on small sets in abelian groups\nAuthors: Simone Costa, Stefano Della Fiore, Mattia Fontana, Lluís Vena\nDate: Sat Mar 21 21:57:00 2026\n\nA famous conjecture of Graham asserts that every set $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{Z}_p \\setminus \\{0\\}$ can be ordered so that all partial sums are distinct. Although this conjecture was recently proved for sufficiently large primes by Pham and Sauermann in~\\cite{PM} (combined with earlier results of \\cite{BBKMM}), it remains open for general abelian groups, even in the cyclic case $\\mathbb{Z}_k$. In this paper, using a recursive approach, we investigate the sequenceability of subsets $A$ in generic abelian groups for small values of $|A|$. We prove that any subset $A \\subseteq G\\setminus\\{0\\}$ with $|A| \\leq 20$ is sequenceable where previously it was known only for $|A|\\leq 9$. This bound is improved to $|A| \\leq 22$ for zero-sum subsets. Finally, regarding the related CMPP conjecture, we show that zero-sum subsets without inverse pairs are sequenceable for $|A| \\leq 23$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCOMBINATORICS OF VEXILLARY (https://ecommons.cornell.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/24fc3ae2-00c5-4d70-81fb-e223b03e2f18/content)\nciteturn4search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Conjecture 2.3.1 ([MSSD22], Conjecture 1.2). ... If S , U(P), then there is some up-elbow tile (i, j) in P which is not in\nconjectured that the supports of Grothendieck polynomials have the following\nproperties.\nConjecture 2.3.1 ([MSSD22], Conjecture 1.2). Let w ∈S n, and let (a1, . . . , an) ∈\nsupp(Gw) such that Pn\nk=1 ak < deg(Gw). Then there exists i ∈[n] such that (a1, . . . , an) +\nei ∈supp(Gw).\nConjecture 2.3.2 ([MSSD22], Conjecture 1.3). Let w ∈S n, and let (a1, . . . , an), (b1, . 